@startuml
!include https://raw.githubusercontent.com/proveo-ca/identity/main/proveo.puml
skinparam noteTextAlignment left
' purpose: E3 — retire the sbx e2e skip, and why `sbx exec` beats `sbx ssh` for it
' covers: e2e/sbx_test.go
' The skip this is about: sbxShellHoldsInADetachedPane, e2e/sbx_test.go:508
'   (declared there; consulted at sbx_test.go:536 and sbx_egress_test.go:83 —
'    the line was 687 when this file was written, re-checked 2026-09-10)
' Sibling experiments: sbx-kit-capabilities.puml (E1 adopted, E2 folded in)
' Notes here are capped at 5 lines x 50 columns; one idea per anchor.
'
' ── UPDATE 2026-09-10: THE SKIP HAS SPREAD, SO E3 GOT MORE EXPENSIVE ───────
' Re-measured, because an experiment nobody references is an experiment nobody
' re-costs. `sbxShellHoldsInADetachedPane` is now consulted from TWO files, not
' one:
'   e2e/sbx_test.go:536        the backend assertion this file was opened for
'   e2e/sbx_egress_test.go:83  TestSandboxEgressIsConfinedToTheKitAllowlist
'
' The second one matters more than the first. That test is the only thing that
' checks a Kit allowlist actually CONFINES, and it already skips on an allow-all
' host baseline (policy-baseline.puml). So it now carries TWO independent skip
' gates, and on a host where either fires, proveo's confinement claim is
' unmeasured — which is exactly the hole kit-domain-form.puml was written out of:
' a malformed allowlist cost nothing visible because nothing was checking.
'
' So E3 is not "retire a skip in one test". It is the precondition for measuring
' confinement at all, and its priority should be read that way.
'
' Also worth naming: this is a HOST-CAPABILITY skip, and all-defs-green.puml's R5
' says a skip must declare which kind it is. This one does say so in its message,
' which is the standard the others should meet.
'
' ── THE SKIP ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
' TestSandboxBackendRunsDockerInsideTheSandbox asserts the four things
' `docker: sbx` claims:
'   1. the run took the sandbox backend, not the docker+egress fallback
'   2. the workspace mount carries writes back to the host
'   3. the sandbox SUPPLIES docker — a daemon answers inside it
'   4. the sandbox is gone afterwards, VM and all
'
' It skips, because sbxShellHoldsInADetachedPane returns false: sbx's own shell
' agent exits within seconds when driven from a detached tmux pane, with proveo
' uninvolved. The probe is deliberately written against SBX rather than proveo
' so it turns back into coverage by itself the day sbx holds.
'
' ── E3 AS PROPOSED WAS `sbx ssh`, AND THAT IS THE WRONG TOOL ────────────────
' The proposal read: `sbx setup ssh` once on the host, then
' `ssh <sandbox>.sbx -- <probe>` instead of a tmux pane. It would work, and it
' is more machinery than the job needs.
'
' `sbx exec` ALREADY DOES THIS, and this repo already uses it. probeLaunch
' (e2e/ladder_test.go:480) runs
'     sbx exec <sandbox> -- sh -c <script>
' against a live sandbox and reads the output, and it has produced real answers
' all session — the shebang/execve probe that eliminated every file-level cause
' of the cecli defect ran through it. No setup step, no ssh client config, no
' second auth path.
'
' ── SO THE REAL QUESTION IS NARROWER ────────────────────────────────────────
' tmux is not there to reach the SANDBOX. It is there to drive PROVEO: the test
' runs `proveo run <target> --shell`, which wants a terminal, and the pane is
' how a test gives it one. sbx's shell agent then dies inside that pane.
'
' Which splits the four claims in two:
'   2 and 3 are about the SANDBOX — what is mounted, what answers inside it.
'     Both are readable with `sbx exec` against a sandbox that already exists,
'     with no pane and no agent session at all.
'   1 and 4 are about PROVEO — which backend it chose, and whether it tore the
'     VM down. Neither needs a session held open either: 1 is visible in
'     `proveo run --print` or the run log, and 4 is `sbx ls` after teardown.
'
' If that holds, the skip is not blocked on sbx holding a pane. It is blocked
' on the test having been written as one session-driving probe when it is four
' independent assertions, only some of which need a session.
'
' ── UNMEASURED, AND THE ORDER TO MEASURE IT ────────────────────────────────
' Every line above is read from this repo; none is run, because sbx is
' host-side and absent here. The cheap first probe is one command:
'     sbx run --name probe -t <image> shell <ws> &   # or however it detaches
'     sbx exec probe -- sh -c 'echo alive; ls /'
' If `sbx exec` reaches a sandbox whose session was never attached to a
' terminal, claims 2 and 3 are free and the ssh route can be dropped
' unmeasured. If it does not, `sbx ssh` becomes worth its setup cost and E3
' reverts to the original proposal.
'
' NOT ADOPTED, NOT REJECTED. Recorded before writing code because the last four
' items in this tree that were built on a plausible reading each cost a rebuild
' cycle, and one of them cost 210 MB an image.
' ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

title Experiment — E3: driving an sbx session from a test

component "e2e/sbx_test.go\nfour claims, one skip" as TEST <<error>>
component "tmux pane\ngives proveo a terminal" as TMUX <<app>>
component "sbx shell agent\nexits in a detached pane" as SHELL <<error>>

component "sbx exec\nalready used by probeLaunch" as EXEC <<db>>
component "sbx ssh\nsetup step + client config" as SSH <<cloud>>

node "the four claims" as C <<host>> {
  component "2 · workspace mount\n3 · docker answers inside" as SBOX <<app>>
  component "1 · backend chosen\n4 · VM gone after" as PRV <<app>>
}

TEST ARROW_MAIN TMUX : needs a terminal for proveo
TMUX ARROW_ERROR SHELL : agent dies here
SHELL ARROW_ERROR TEST : so the whole probe skips
EXEC ARROW_MAIN SBOX : readable with no pane
EXEC ARROW_QUEUE PRV : run log · sbx ls
SSH ARROW_QUEUE SBOX : would also work, costs more

note bottom of EXEC
  **The tool is already in the tree.** probeLaunch
  runs `sbx exec <sandbox> -- sh -c` and produced
  the shebang evidence that cleared every
  file-level cause of the cecli defect, so its
  reach into a live sandbox is measured.
end note

note bottom of TMUX
  **tmux is for proveo, not for sbx.** The pane
  exists because `proveo run --shell` wants a
  terminal; sbx's agent merely dies inside it.
  Naming the wrong owner is what made this look
  like an sbx limitation.
end note

note bottom of SBOX
  **Two claims need no session at all.** What is
  mounted and what answers inside are properties
  of the sandbox, readable once it exists —
  which is why the skip may be a test-shape
  problem rather than a runtime one.
end note

note bottom of TEST
  **A suite that skips reports success.** This
  one skips precisely where the shipping backend
  lives, so the gap sits under the path proveo
  actually takes, and no regression there would
  be caught.
end note

note bottom of SSH
  **Right answer to a question not asked.** ssh
  reaches a sandbox, but so does exec with no
  setup, no client config and no second auth
  path — so it is the fallback, not the plan.
end note

@enduml
